.TH EUCLID-CLI-EKV-QUERY 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.73" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ekv-query \- read one partition's items, in sort-key order
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli ekv query
.BR \-\-table | \-t
.I TABLE
.BR \-\-partition\-key | \-p
.I JSON
[\fB\-\-operator\fR|\fB\-o\fR \fIOP\fR \fB\-\-value\fR|\fB\-v\fR \fIJSON\fR]
[\fB\-\-upper\fR|\fB\-u\fR \fIJSON\fR]
[\fB\-\-descending\fR|\fB\-d\fR]
[\fB\-\-page\-size\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-page\-index\fR \fIN\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Reads the items of one partition, in sort-key order.
.PP
A query always names one partition exactly \- that is what makes it a
query rather than a scan, and what lets the database walk an index
instead of reading the table. Without an operator the whole partition
comes back; with one, the part of it the sort key selects.
.PP
Every operator is a bound on the sort key, answered from the index in
order. A table with no sort key has nothing to narrow by, and asking it
to is an error rather than a query that quietly ignores half of what it
was told.
.SH VALUES ARE JSON
The partition key and the bounds are given as JSON, so a string is
quoted and a number is not:
.PP
.RS
.nf
--partition-key '"4711"'    # against a string key
--partition-key 4711        # against a number key
.fi
.RE
.PP
The single quotes are the shell's, keeping the JSON quotes intact. A
value of the wrong type is refused: it would otherwise match nothing,
which looks exactly like an empty partition.
.SH OPERATORS
.TP
.B eq
The sort key equals the value.
.TP
.BR lt ", " le ", " gt ", " ge
Less than, at most, greater than, at least.
.TP
.B between
From
.B \-\-value
to
.BR \-\-upper ,
both included.
.TP
.B begins-with
The sort key starts with the value. Strings only \- a prefix of a
number is not a thing, and asking for one is an error rather than
nonsense.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-table " " \fITABLE\fR
Table to query. Required.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-partition\-key " " \fIJSON\fR
The partition key's value. Required.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-operator " " \fIOP\fR
How to narrow by sort key. Needs a
.BR \-\-value .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-value " " \fIJSON\fR
What to compare the sort key against; the lower bound for
.BR between .
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-upper " " \fIJSON\fR
The upper bound, for
.B between
only.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-descending
Read in descending sort-key order \- newest first, where the sort key
is a timestamp.
.TP
.BR \-\-page\-size " " \fIN\fR
Most to return.
.TP
.BR \-\-page\-index " " \fIN\fR
Zero-based page, applied when a page size is given.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The items are printed, with how many came back.
.TP
.B 1
No such table, the partition key was of the wrong type, the operator
was not one of the seven, an operator was given without a value, the
table has no sort key to narrow by, or
.B begins-with
was asked of a key that is not a string.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
# Everything one supplier ever delivered, oldest first
euclid-cli ekv query --table deliveries --partition-key '"4711"'

# The most recent delivery
euclid-cli ekv query -t deliveries -p '"4711"' --descending --page-size 1

# One month of them
euclid-cli ekv query -t deliveries -p '"4711"' \e
    --operator begins-with --value '"2026-09"'

# A window, both ends included
euclid-cli ekv query -t deliveries -p '"4711"' --operator between \e
    --value '"2026-09-01"' --upper '"2026-09-30T23:59:59Z"'

# Versions after 9, ordered as numbers
euclid-cli ekv query -t versions -p '"handbook"' --operator gt --value 9
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-ekv (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekv-create-table (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekv-get-item (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekv-scan (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Jens Vogt <jens.vogt@opitz-consulting.com>
